   <dc:creator>
    <rdf:Seq>
     <rdf:li>Diego HernÃ¡n Villarejo-GarcÃ­a</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li>Carlos Navarro-MartÃ­nez</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li>JosÃ© Francisco LÃ³pez-Gil and JosÃ© Pino-Ortega</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Seq>
   </dc:creator>
   <dc:title>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Birth Quarter</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Birth Quarter</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Playing Position</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Playing Position</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and Market Value in Elite International Football: Evidence from the FIFA World Cup 2026</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and Market Value in Elite International Football: Evidence from the FIFA World Cup 2026</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:title>
   <dc:subject>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>relative age effect; birth quarter; elite football; playing position; market value; FIFA World Cup; multilevel modelling</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:subject>
   <dc:type>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>Text</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:type>
   <dc:description>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">The relative age effect is well documented in football</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">The relative age effect is well documented in football</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">but unequal birth-quarter representation does not necessarily translate into positional or economic differences among elite players. This study examined associations between birth quarter</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">but unequal birth-quarter representation does not necessarily translate into positional or economic differences among elite players. This study examined associations between birth quarter</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">playing position</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">playing position</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and market value among 1248 players from the 48 national teams participating in the 2026 FIFA World Cup. Publicly available FIFA and Transfermarkt data</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and market value among 1248 players from the 48 national teams participating in the 2026 FIFA World Cup. Publicly available FIFA and Transfermarkt data</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">with market valuations retrieved on 10 August 2026</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">with market valuations retrieved on 10 August 2026</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">were analysed using chi-square goodness-of-fit tests</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">were analysed using chi-square goodness-of-fit tests</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">multivariable logistic regression</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">multivariable logistic regression</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and linear models adjusted for age</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and linear models adjusted for age</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">height</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">height</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">continental confederation</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">continental confederation</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">for market value</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">for market value</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">age2 and playing position. Economic analyses included 827 players and incorporated a national-team random intercept. Players born early in the year were overrepresented</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">age2 and playing position. Economic analyses included 827 players and incorporated a national-team random intercept. Players born early in the year were overrepresented</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">but no birth-quarter comparisons in the primary adjusted models reached statistical significance for playing position or market value. For goalkeepers</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">but no birth-quarter comparisons in the primary adjusted models reached statistical significance for playing position or market value. For goalkeepers</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">the comparison between the fourth (Q4) and first (Q1) birth quarters yielded an odds ratio of 0.53 (95% confidence interval: 0.28--1.01). Adjusted market-value differences relative to Q1 were +15.9% for the second birth quarter (Q2)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">the comparison between the fourth (Q4) and first (Q1) birth quarters yielded an odds ratio of 0.53 (95% confidence interval: 0.28--1.01). Adjusted market-value differences relative to Q1 were +15.9% for the second birth quarter (Q2)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">$-$1.4% for the third (Q3)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">$-$1.4% for the third (Q3)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and +10.7% for Q4. National-team clustering accounted for 35.2% of the residual variance in log-transformed market value not explained by the fixed effects. These findings distinguish unequal representation from adjusted positional and economic associations within an already selected elite sample. However</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and +10.7% for Q4. National-team clustering accounted for 35.2% of the residual variance in log-transformed market value not explained by the fixed effects. These findings distinguish unequal representation from adjusted positional and economic associations within an already selected elite sample. However</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">confidence intervals included potentially relevant associations</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">confidence intervals included potentially relevant associations</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and the cross-sectional design cannot establish how relative age influences earlier selection and progression.</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and the cross-sectional design cannot establish how relative age influences earlier selection and progression.</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:description>
